<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islam Shah Suri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1545–1554</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Islam Shah Sur, Jalal Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Shah Suri was the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, reigning from 1545 to 1554 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>. He kept his father's administration together and strengthened central control over the Afghan nobles. His death in 1554 led to succession struggles that broke the empire apart and allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1555.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri.jpg" alt="Islam Shah Suri" width="320" height="540" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islam_Shah_Suri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
